: 0.0, "money": -0.33}, "rating_margin": 0.25, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.78, "baseline_margin": 1.54, "analyst_adjustment": 0.46, "key_factors": ["Strong Democratic national environment offsets the district's four-point Republican lean", "Dandiya holds a large cash advantage ($1.3M to $89K) with DCCC ads attacking Askar in the closing weeks", "Raters overwhelmingly call it a Tossup; prediction markets give Democrats a modest 54-62% edge", "Polling is sparse, dated (last usable poll late August), and all Democratic-sponsored"], "rationale": "An R+4 open seat in a strongly favorable Democratic environment leaves this essentially even on fundamentals, and nearly every rater calls it a Tossup. Dandiya's decisive cash-on-hand edge plus Democratic outside advertising against Askar, alongside prediction markets tilting her way, justify a narrow Democratic edge rather than a coin flip.", "watch": "A late Trump-assisted turnout surge for Askar, or fresh polling showing movement either way, could break the deadlock.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Newest usable poll is 46 days old; polling uncertainty inflated."]}, "overview": "Florida's 22nd District is an open-seat contest between Republican Casey Askar and Democrat Pia Dandiya in a district that backed Republicans by four points at the presidential level. A strong national Democratic environment offsets that lean, and most professional raters call the race a Tossup, with two placing it as a slight Democratic edge and two as a slight Republican edge.\n\nMoney tells a clearer story: Dandiya enters the final weeks with roughly $1.3 million on hand against Askar's $89,000, and Democratic groups have launched ads attacking Askar and courting Hispanic voters, while a gun-safety group has added air support. Prediction markets give Democrats a modest edge, and the sparse, older polls show a dead heat. A Trump rally in nearby Fort Myers could sharpen Republican turnout in a race this close.", "history": [{"date": "20